WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:07.980
From untouched coastline to tropical forests and age-old fishing villages, the best

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.640
place to behold the jewels of Zanzibar is
from the sky.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.520
But now we also know how quick those jewels
are disappearing.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.050
Computer scientists from the University of
Zanzibar are using drones to take pictures

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.359
of every centimeter of the isles.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:31.449
They hope the images will encourage people
to pursue more responsible developments.

00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:34.909
“We believe there are thresholds to the
environment.

00:00:34.909 --> 00:00:36.199
There are limits.

00:00:36.199 --> 00:00:42.150
You may have the numbers of hotels, but if
you don’t where they are, or what interference

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:50.680
they have on the water and the people, and
what was before - maybe there was a forest?

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.830
So the information is good - because we want to balance. We're talking about responsible tourism.”

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.280
“The maps that these drones create are
more than 25 times more detailed than those

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:04.350
of Google.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.830
It allows these technicians to see Zanzibar in a way no scientist nor Government has ever

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.280
seen before.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.190
And they hope that will expand these
isles horizons.”

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.100
The tens of thousands of drone photos are
meshed together to create stunning 3D maps.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.020
They could help authorities work out where
buildings are going up too fast.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.960
Like this area, where they took two images
just six months apart.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.940
“So if you look in a small area like this
one.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.490
And then we came up to take another image
to see what has changed.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.190
So the green is running out in Zanzibar - we
want to prevent that.”

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:45.850
“It changed me to understand that I need
to do something for my myself.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:51.320
I need to tell people that how we are building
- it’s really not good.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.220
We are destroying our own environment.”

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.340
It is not only a question of beauty.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.960
Poor infrastructure and planning ruins lives
in Zanzibar.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:07.160
Annual floods frequently kill people and submerge
neighbourhoods for days on end.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:16.020
“I thank god the floods haven’t killed
anybody in our neighbourhood.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.790
But they destroy our property and people have
to leave their houses.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:23.010
They lose possessions, beds, all sorts of
things.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.400
And we are poor people, it takes us a long
time to recover.”

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.360
The researchers say they will supply their
maps to all relevant authorities

00:02:31.363 --> 00:02:34.230
- and empower communities to hold people accountable.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:40.470
So while beauty lies in the eye of the beholder,
Its preservation may lie much higher up.

